NAME
       wc - count lines, words and characters

SYNOPSIS
       wc [-lwc] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       wc counts the newlines, words and bytes in each file, or in
       the standard input if no file is named, and prints them in
       that order, each in a 7-column field, followed by the file
       name. A word is a maximal run of characters other than
       space, tab and newline.

       With more than one file a last line gives the totals, named
       total. A file named - is the standard input.

OPTIONS
       The letters may be combined in one argument (e.g. -lw) or
       given separately (-l -w); -- ends the options. The counts
       always come out in the order lines, words, bytes. An unknown
       option is a usage error.

       -l     count lines
       -w     count words
       -c     count characters (bytes)

EXAMPLES
       wc /etc/passwd                lines, words, bytes
       ls | wc -l                    how many files here
       wc -l *.c                     lines in each, and in all

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every file could be read; 1 if one could not be opened
       (wc says why and goes on), or for a usage error.

SEE ALSO
       grep(1), sort(1)

BUGS
       Only space, tab and newline separate words. There is no -m
       (characters as distinct from bytes) and no -L.
